We encountered an issue with our Nginx and PHP-FPM setup on the Kubernetes cluster this morning, which halted its functionality. 
Investigate and rectify the issue:

The pod name is nginx-phpfpm and configmap name is nginx-config. Identify and fix the problem.

Once resolved, copy /home/thor/index.php file from the jump host to the nginx-container within the nginx document root. After this, you should be able to access the website using Website button on the top bar.

Note: The kubectl utility on the jump-host has been configured to work with the Kubernetes cluster.

SOLUTION
The issue stems from a path mismatch between the Nginx configuration and the volume mount path in the nginx-container. The ConfigMap nginx-config likely sets the document root to /var/www/html, but the nginx-container is mounting the shared volume at the default Nginx path (e.g., /usr/share/nginx/html), causing Nginx to look in the wrong directory for the PHP file.

🔧 Step 1: Fix the Volume Mount Path
You need to edit the pod definition so the nginx-container mounts the shared volume at the correct location.

Export the current pod configuration:

bash
kubectl get pod nginx-phpfpm -o yaml > nginx-phpfpm.yaml
Edit the nginx-phpfpm.yaml file. Locate the volumeMounts section for the nginx-container and change the mountPath to match the path specified in the nginx-config ConfigMap (typically /var/www/html):

yaml
# Under the nginx-container spec:
volumeMounts:
- name: shared-files
  mountPath: /var/www/html   # Change this to match the ConfigMap root
Apply the change by recreating the pod:

bash
kubectl delete pod nginx-phpfpm
kubectl apply -f nginx-phpfpm.yaml
📂 Step 2: Copy the PHP File
Once the pod is running again, copy the file from the jump host into the specific container.

bash
kubectl cp /home/thor/index.php nginx-phpfpm:/var/www/html/index.php -c nginx-container
After this, the Nginx container should be able to serve the index.php file from the shared volume, and the website should be accessible.

